(window.LADDER_CARDS["23a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["23a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"23a1.1","q":"What is f⁽ⁿ⁾, when is a function smooth, and what does the kth slope of p(x) = Σ uₖ(x − w)ᵏ give at w?","a":"f⁽ⁿ⁾ is the nth slope of f, with f⁽⁰⁾ = f; f is smooth on an open interval when it has slopes of every order there; the kth slope of p at w is k!·uₖ, since lower powers have been sloped away and higher ones still carry a factor (x − w) that is 0 at w","w":["f⁽ⁿ⁾ is the nth power of f; f is smooth when it is continuous; the kth slope of p at w is uₖ","f⁽ⁿ⁾ is the nth slope of f; f is smooth when it has a first slope; the kth slope of p at w is k·uₖ","f⁽ⁿ⁾ is the nth slope of f; f is smooth when it has slopes of every order; the kth slope of p at w is the sum of all the uⱼ"],"why":"(x − w)ᵏ has kth slope k!, a constant, so the slope at w picks out one term."},
 {"u":"23a2.1","q":"If f has n slopes at w, how many polynomials of degree at most n agree with f and its first n slopes at w, and why does each new term leave the earlier slopes alone?","a":"exactly one: Tₙ(x) = Σ f⁽ᵏ⁾(w)(x − w)ᵏ/k!, each coefficient forced by k!·uₖ = f⁽ᵏ⁾(w); the term in (x − w)ᵏ has every slope below the kth equal to 0 at w","w":["endlessly many, one for each choice of the last coefficient; a new term changes every slope a little","exactly one, Tₙ(x) = Σ f⁽ᵏ⁾(w)(x − w)ᵏ, with no factorials; the new term is 0 everywhere","none, unless f is a polynomial; a new term is added only when f is one"],"why":"So a better match is made by adding a term, never by redoing the ones before."},
 {"u":"23a3.1","q":"What are the matches at 0 for eˣ, sin, cos and ln(1 + x), and why does sin keep only odd powers?","a":"eˣ: 1 + x + x²/2! + ⋯; sin: x − x³/3! + x⁵/5! − ⋯; cos: 1 − x²/2! + x⁴/4! − ⋯; ln(1 + x): x − x²/2 + x³/3 − ⋯; the slopes of sin at 0 cycle 0, 1, 0, −1, so every even one is 0, and for cos every odd one","w":["eˣ: 1 + x + x² + ⋯; sin: x + x³/3! + ⋯; cos: 1 + x²/2! + ⋯; ln(1 + x): x + x²/2 + ⋯; sin keeps odd powers because it is positive","eˣ: 1 + x + x²/2! + ⋯; sin: 1 − x²/2! + ⋯; cos: x − x³/3! + ⋯; ln(1 + x): x − x²/2! + x³/3! − ⋯; sin keeps even powers","eˣ: x + x²/2 + ⋯; sin: x − x³/3 + x⁵/5 − ⋯; cos: 1 − x²/2 + x⁴/4 − ⋯; ln(1 + x): 1 − x + x² − ⋯; the odd powers come from the chain technique"],"why":"exp is its own slope, so every slope at 0 is 1; the slopes of sin and cos cycle in four."},
 {"u":"23a4.1","q":"What is out of place in “Tₙ(x) = Σ f⁽ᵏ⁾(x)(x − w)ᵏ/k!”, and in “f and Tₙ agree at w, so they agree near w”?","a":"the slopes are taken at w and then frozen; they do not move with x; and the match is made at w only: how near it holds is the question of the error, 23b's","w":["the factorials should be k, not k!; and agreement at w gives agreement everywhere","the powers should be xᵏ; and nothing is out of place in the second","the slopes are taken at x; and f and Tₙ never agree, even at w"],"why":"A match is a statement about one point until an error bound says more."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["23b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["23b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"23b1.1","q":"What is left over when f is matched to degree n, what is the trick of the proof, and what closes it?","a":"Rₙ(x) = f⁽ⁿ⁺¹⁾(c)(x − w)ⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1)! for some c between w and x; slide the point of matching from w to x, with a number M chosen so the sliding error g is 0 at both ends; level ends give a level point between (14b)","w":["Rₙ(x) = f⁽ⁿ⁺¹⁾(w)(x − w)ⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1)!; the trick is to take one more slope at w; the squeeze closes it","Rₙ(x) = f⁽ⁿ⁾(c)(x − w)ⁿ/n! for some c; the trick is induction on n; the climb technique closes it","Rₙ(x) = 0 for smooth f; the trick is to let n grow; the ratio test closes it"],"why":"g′(c) = 0 forces M = f⁽ⁿ⁺¹⁾(c)/(n + 1)!."},
 {"u":"23b2.1","q":"What does a ceiling K on |f⁽ⁿ⁺¹⁾| between w and x give, and why do the matches of sin, cos and eˣ reach the function at every x?","a":"|Rₙ(x)| ≤ K|x − w|ⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1)!; every slope of sin and cos is at most 1 in size and those of eˣ at most e^|x|, and |x|ⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1)! heads to 0 for every x","w":["|Rₙ(x)| ≤ K|x − w|ⁿ⁺¹; the matches reach the function only for |x| < 1","|Rₙ(x)| ≤ K/(n + 1)!; the matches reach the function because the functions are smooth","|Rₙ(x)| ≤ K|x − w|ⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1)!; the matches reach the function because K heads to 0"],"why":"xⁿ/n! → 0 for any x (22h2): factorials outgrow powers."},
 {"u":"23b3.1","q":"For which x does the ceiling xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) show that x − x²/2 + x³/3 − ⋯ reaches ln(1 + x), and where does it give out?","a":"for 0 ≤ x ≤ 1, where it is at most 1/(n + 1); for x > 1 the pieces do not head to 0 and the sum wanders; for −1 < x < 0 the sum does reach ln(1 + x), but the ceiling is too rough to show it, since c can sit near −1","w":["for every x > −1, since ln(1 + x) is smooth there","for 0 ≤ x < 1 only; at x = 1 the sum wanders","for −1 < x < 0 only; for x ≥ 0 the ceiling grows"],"why":"In particular ln 2 = 1 − ½ + ⅓ − ⋯."},
 {"u":"23b5.1","q":"What is out of place in “Rₙ(x) = f⁽ⁿ⁺¹⁾(w)(x − w)ⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1)!”, and in “f smooth, so Tₙ(x) → f(x)”?","a":"the slope is at some c between w and x, not at w; and the error must be shown to head to 0: ln(1 + x) is smooth at x = 2 and its sum wanders there","w":["the factorial should be n!; and smooth functions always equal their sums","the slope is at x; and Tₙ(x) → f(x) fails only for functions with a corner","nothing in the first; in the second f must be a polynomial"],"why":"Smoothness gives the matches; only an error bound says they arrive."},
 {"u":"23b4.1","q":"What does the middle-slope technique bound, and by what? Why is h chosen as √(M₀/M₂)?","a":"|f′| by 2√(M₀M₂), where M₀ bounds |f| and M₂ bounds |f″|; that h makes M₀/h and hM₂ equal, which gives the least total","w":["|f″| by M₀ + M₁; that h makes the error 0","|f| by the product of its slopes; any h gives the same bound","|f′| by M₀M₂; that h is the largest allowed"],"why":"23b4: f′(x) is a chord slope over 2h less h f″(c), so |f′(x)| ≤ M₀/h + hM₂ for every h > 0."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["23c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["23c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"23c1.1","q":"What is a power sum about w, and what follows if it settles at x₀?","a":"Σ uₖ(x − w)ᵏ with real coefficients; if it settles at x₀ it settles in size at every x nearer to w than x₀ is: its pieces at x₀ are bounded by some B, so the pieces at x are at most B·rᵏ with r = |x − w|/|x₀ − w| < 1, a geometric comparison","w":["a polynomial in (x − w); if it settles at x₀ it settles at every x","Σ uₖ(x − w)ᵏ; if it settles at x₀ it settles at every x farther from w than x₀","Σ uₖ(x − w)ᵏ; if it settles at x₀ it settles at −x₀ as well, by comparison with the harmonic sum"],"why":"The pieces at x₀ head to 0, and a list that settles has a floor and a ceiling."},
 {"u":"23c2.1","q":"What is the reach R of a power sum about w, and where does it come from?","a":"R is the least upper bound of the distances |x − w| at which the sum settles, or no ceiling at all; the sum settles in size where |x − w| < R and wanders where |x − w| > R","w":["R is the largest x at which the sum settles; the sum settles for every x ≤ R","R is the first coefficient that is 0; the sum is a polynomial of degree R","R is the least upper bound of the coefficients; the sum settles where |uₖ| < R"],"why":"The set of such distances holds 0, and 8c gives its least upper bound when it has a ceiling."},
 {"u":"23c3.1","q":"How is the reach read from the ratio |uₖ₊₁/uₖ|?","a":"if it heads to L the reach is 1/L; if it heads to 0 the sum reaches everywhere; if it passes every ceiling the reach is 0","w":["if it heads to L the reach is L; if it heads to 0 the reach is 0","if it heads to L the reach is 1/L; if it heads to 0 the reach is 0; if it passes every ceiling the sum reaches everywhere","if it heads to L the reach is 1 − L, whatever L is"],"why":"Run the ratio test on the sizes of the pieces: the ratios head to L|x − w|."},
 {"u":"23c4.1","q":"What can happen at the edges of the reach, and why does the sum for 1/(1 + x²) stop at reach 1?","a":"anything: Σ xᵏ wanders at both, Σ xᵏ/k wanders at 1 and settles at −1, Σ xᵏ/k² settles at both; nothing on the real line explains the stop at 1: the reason lives off the line, where 1 + x² is 0 one unit from the centre, and rung 25 builds that place","w":["the sum always wanders at both edges; the sum for 1/(1 + x²) stops at 1 because the function has a corner there","the sum always settles at both edges; the sum for 1/(1 + x²) stops at 1 because its coefficients grow","anything; the sum for 1/(1 + x²) stops at 1 because the function is 0 at x = 1"],"why":"1/(1 + x²) is smooth along the whole line, and at x = 2 it is a fifth while the sum wanders."},
 {"u":"23c6.1","q":"What is out of place in “R = 1, so the sum settles at x = 1”, and in “|uₖ₊₁/uₖ| has no limit, so there is no reach”?","a":"the edges are open: anything can happen there; and every power sum has a reach (23c2): the ratio is one way to find it, not the reach itself","w":["the sum settles at x = 1 only if R > 1; and a sum with no ratio limit has reach 0","nothing in the first; in the second the reach is the largest ratio","R = 1 means the sum wanders at 1; and no ratio limit means the sum reaches everywhere"],"why":"The reach comes from a least upper bound, which needs no ratio."},
 {"u":"23c5.1","q":"What does the from-inside technique say? Why does it not give 1 − 1 + 1 − ⋯ the total ½?","a":"if a power sum settles at the end of its reach, the function inside heads to that total; the technique starts from a sum that settles at the edge, and 1 − 1 + 1 − ⋯ does not","w":["every power sum settles at the end of its reach; 1 − 1 + 1 − ⋯ has no reach","if the function inside has a limit at the edge, the sum settles there; ½ is not a whole number","a power sum is 0 at the end of its reach; the pieces are not positive"],"why":"23c5: f(x) − s is (1 − x) Σ (sₙ − s)xⁿ, and the early part shrinks with 1 − x while the far part is under ε."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["23d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["23d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"23d1.1","q":"What does it mean for functions f₁, f₂, … to settle at each point to f, and what does xⁿ on [0, 1] show?","a":"for every x in D the list of reals fₙ(x) settles at f(x); xⁿ settles to 0 for x < 1 and to 1 at x = 1: every fₙ is continuous and the limit jumps","w":["some x in D has fₙ(x) settling at f(x); xⁿ shows the limit of continuous functions is continuous","for every x in D the list fₙ(x) settles at f(x); xⁿ settles to 0 at every point of [0, 1]","the areas of the fₙ settle; xⁿ shows the areas head to 1"],"why":"As a sum, (1 − x) + (x − x²) + ⋯ has continuous pieces and a total that jumps at 1."},
 {"u":"23d2.1","q":"How does settling everywhere at once differ from settling at each point, and why does xⁿ settle at once on [0, r] with r < 1 but not on [0, 1]?","a":"only the order of “every x” and “some N” changes: at once, one N for each ε works for every x; on [0, r] the worst point is r and rⁿ → 0; on [0, 1] the point xₙ = (½)^(1/n) has xₙⁿ = ½, so no N answers ε = ¼","w":["at once means one ε works for every N; xⁿ settles at once on [0, 1] because it is continuous","at once means every x has its own N; xⁿ fails on [0, r] because rⁿ grows","only the order changes; xⁿ fails on [0, 1] because it does not settle at the point 1"],"why":"10a warned that swapping that order changes the claim."},
 {"u":"23d3.1","q":"What does settling everywhere at once keep, and which line of the proof spends “at once”?","a":"continuity: if each fₙ is continuous at w and fₙ settles at once to f near w, f is continuous at w; the first line, which chooses one n good at x and at w together, so that ε splits in three through that single fₙ","w":["slopes: f has the limit of the slopes; the last line spends it","continuity; the middle line, where fₙ is continuous at w, spends it","areas only; no line spends it, since settling at each point would do"],"why":"Settling at each point would give an n for x and another for w, and no single fₙ to compare through."},
 {"u":"23d4.1","q":"When does Σ fₖ settle everywhere at once on D, and what does that give for a power sum inside its reach?","a":"when |fₖ(x)| ≤ Mₖ for every x in D and Σ Mₖ settles: the tail is bounded with no x left in it; a power sum settles at once on [w − r, w + r] for each r < R, so it is continuous inside its reach","w":["when each fₖ is continuous; a power sum is continuous everywhere","when Σ fₖ(x) settles for each x; a power sum settles at once on its whole reach, edges included","when |fₖ(x)| ≤ Mₖ and Mₖ → 0; a power sum is continuous at its edges"],"why":"Take Mₖ = |uₖ|rᵏ: r is inside the reach, so that sum settles."},
 {"u":"23d5.1","q":"What is out of place in “each fₙ continuous and fₙ → f at each point, so f is continuous”, and in “at once on [0, r] for each r < 1, so at once on [0, 1)”?","a":"settling at each point does not keep continuity: xⁿ on [0, 1]; and each r has its own N, with no one N for all r < 1 together: xⁿ again","w":["the fₙ must be smooth; and [0, 1) is not an interval","nothing in the first; in the second the interval must be closed","settling at each point keeps continuity only on closed intervals; and at once on each [0, r] gives at once on [0, 1]"],"why":"Both mistakes swap “every” and “some” once more."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["23e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["23e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"23e1.1","q":"Continuous fₙ settle everywhere at once to f on [p, q]. What follows for areas, and what does n²x(1 − x)ⁿ show?","a":"∫ fₙ heads to ∫ f, since one N bounds the gap at every input and so bounds the gap in areas; n²x(1 − x)ⁿ settles at each point to 0 on [0, 1] while its areas head to 1: at each point is not enough","w":["∫ fₙ equals ∫ f for every n; n²x(1 − x)ⁿ shows the areas head to 0","∫ fₙ heads to ∫ f; n²x(1 − x)ⁿ settles at once to 0 and its areas head to 0","nothing follows for areas; n²x(1 − x)ⁿ shows areas never pass to the limit"],"why":"The gap in areas is at most ε(q − p)."},
 {"u":"23e2.1","q":"How do the reach of Σ uₖ(x − w)ᵏ and of its slope sum Σ k uₖ(x − w)ᵏ⁻¹ compare, and which direction is the easier?","a":"they are the same; from the slope sum back to the sum is easier: |uₖ(x − w)ᵏ| ≤ |x − w|·|k uₖ(x − w)ᵏ⁻¹| once k ≥ 1, a plain comparison","w":["the slope sum reaches less far, since the factor k makes the pieces grow","they are the same; from the sum to the slope sum is easier, since slopes are smaller","the slope sum reaches one unit farther"],"why":"The other direction must absorb the factor k, which powers of a ratio below 1 can do."},
 {"u":"23e3.1","q":"Inside its reach, how is a power sum sloped and its area taken, and what does that make of it?","a":"term by term: f′(x) = Σ k uₖ(x − w)ᵏ⁻¹ and ∫_wˣ f = Σ uₖ(x − w)ᵏ⁺¹/(k + 1); so a power sum is smooth inside its reach, and uₖ = f⁽ᵏ⁾(w)/k!: it is its own matching sum","w":["term by term, at the edges as well; so a power sum is a polynomial","only its first n terms may be sloped; so a power sum has n slopes","term by term; so two different power sums about w can have the same total near w"],"why":"One total near w has only one power sum about w."},
 {"u":"23e4.1","q":"How are the sums for ln(1 + x) and arctan x reached from the geometric sum, and why is π/4 = 1 − ⅓ + ⅕ − ⋯ nearly useless for computing?","a":"take the area of the finite geometric sum for 1/(1 + t) and for 1/(1 + t²), with an exact leftover that heads to 0; at x = 1 the leftover is about 1/(2n + 1), so a thousand pieces give about three places","w":["take the slope of the geometric sum; the sum for π/4 is useless because it wanders","multiply two geometric sums; the sum for π/4 is useless because its pieces grow","take the area of the geometric sum; the sum for π/4 is useless because π is not a fraction"],"why":"At x = 1/√3, where arctan is π/6, each piece shrinks by a third."},
 {"u":"23e5.1","q":"What is out of place in “the slope sum settles at the edge because the sum does”, and in reordering 1 − ⅓ + ⅕ − ⋯ to compute π/4 faster?","a":"same reach says nothing of the edges: Σ xᵏ/k² settles at 1 and its slope sum is the harmonic sum there; and the sum settles only by taking turns, so a reordering can change its total (22g3)","w":["the slope sum never settles where the sum does; and reordering is allowed but slow","nothing in the first; in the second the pieces must first be doubled","the sum must be sloped twice; and reordering changes only the speed, never the total"],"why":"Edges and orders are where care is spent."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["23f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["23f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"23f1.1","q":"What is shown about f(x) = e^(−1/x²) near 0, and which technique carries it?","a":"f(x)/xᵐ heads to 0 as x heads to 0, for every m: f is flatter than any power; with t = 1/|x| it is tᵐ/e^(t²), and powers are small beside exponentials (22k2)","w":["f(x)/xᵐ heads to 1 for every m; the ratio test carries it","f(x) = 0 near 0; the squeeze carries it","f(x)/x heads to 0 but f(x)/x² does not; the product technique carries it"],"why":"f shrinks faster than x, than x², than x¹⁰⁰⁰."},
 {"u":"23f2.1","q":"What are the slopes of f(x) = e^(−1/x²) at 0, with f(0) = 0, and why?","a":"f is smooth and f⁽ⁿ⁾(0) = 0 for every n: away from 0 each slope is a polynomial in 1/x times e^(−1/x²), and the slope quotient at 0 is again such a product, which 23f1 sends to 0","w":["f has no slope at 0, since 1/x² is not defined there","f′(0) = 0 and the higher slopes at 0 pass every bound","f⁽ⁿ⁾(0) = n! for every n, by 23a1"],"why":"Induction keeps the shape Pₙ(1/x)·e^(−1/x²)."},
 {"u":"23f3.1","q":"What does it mean for f to be rebuilt from w, and why is e^(−1/x²) smooth at 0 and not rebuilt from 0?","a":"some power sum about w with reach above 0 has total f(x) for every x near enough to w; every slope of e^(−1/x²) at 0 is 0, so its matching sum is 0 everywhere, while f(x) > 0 for every x ≠ 0","w":["f has slopes of every order at w; e^(−1/x²) is not smooth at 0","f equals a polynomial near w; e^(−1/x²) is rebuilt from 0 by the polynomial 0","some power sum about w settles; e^(−1/x²) is not rebuilt from 0 because its matching sum wanders"],"why":"Infinitely many smooth functions share every slope at a point."},
 {"u":"23f4.1","q":"What is out of place in “f smooth at w, so f is rebuilt from w”, and in “the matching sum settles at x, so it settles at f(x)”?","a":"e^(−1/x²) at 0 is smooth and not rebuilt; and its matching sum settles at 0 for every x, where f(x) is not 0","w":["smooth functions are rebuilt only at 0; and a matching sum never settles","nothing in the first; in the second the sum must settle in size","f must be a polynomial; and the matching sum settles at f(x) + 1"],"why":"Settling and settling at f are two claims."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["23g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["23g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"23g1.1","q":"Why is e no fraction p/q, and why does the leftover of the sum for e, times q!, land strictly between 0 and 1?","a":"if e = p/q then x = q!(e − Σ_{k ≤ q} 1/k!) would be whole; but x = 1/(q + 1) + 1/((q + 1)(q + 2)) + ⋯ is above 0 and below the geometric sum 1/(q + 1) + 1/(q + 1)² + ⋯ = 1/q ≤ 1","w":["e's decimal never repeats in the places computed; the leftover is under 1 because e < 3","if e = p/q then q! e is not whole; the leftover is 0","e is a limit, and limits are never fractions; the leftover is between 0 and 1 because 1/k! is"],"why":"No whole number lies strictly between 0 and 1. The sum for e settles fast enough for this."},
 {"u":"23g2.1","q":"Suppose π = p/q and f(x) = xⁿ(p − qx)ⁿ/n!. Which two facts about ∫₀^π f(x) sin x dx clash?","a":"it is a whole number, because every slope of f at 0 and at π is whole; and it is above 0 and below 1 once n is large, because f is small on [0, π]","w":["it is 0, because f(π − x) = f(x); and it is positive","it is a fraction with bottom n!; and it passes every bound as n grows","it equals π; and it is whole"],"why":"Again a whole number strictly between 0 and 1."},
 {"u":"23g3.1","q":"What are algebraic and transcendental reals, what is held in trust, and which old question does it settle?","a":"algebraic: a root of a polynomial with whole coefficients, not all 0; transcendental: not; held in trust: e and π are transcendental; ruler and compass build only algebraic lengths, and squaring the circle needs √π, so no recipe squares the circle","w":["algebraic: a fraction; transcendental: not a fraction; held in trust: √2 is transcendental; it settles doubling the cube","algebraic: a root of a polynomial with whole coefficients; held in trust: e + π is a fraction; it settles nothing old","algebraic: built by ruler and compass; transcendental: built by sums; held in trust: π is algebraic; it squares the circle"],"why":"√2 is irrational and algebraic: a root of x² − 2 = 0."},
 {"u":"23g5.1","q":"What is out of place in “π never repeats in 10 million digits, so it is irrational”, and in “e and π are irrational, so e + π is”?","a":"checking digits proves nothing about the rest; the proof does; and irrationals can add to a fraction, as √2 and −√2 add to 0: for e + π nobody knows","w":["10 million digits are too few, a billion would do; and e + π is known to be a fraction","nothing in the first; in the second e + π is irrational because both are positive","π repeats after 10 million digits; and the sum of irrationals is always irrational"],"why":"A count of cases proves no statement."},
 {"u":"23g4.1","q":"What would a whole polynomial with root e force on the number J? How is the plan like the proof that e is not a fraction?","a":"J would be a whole multiple of (p − 1)! that is not 0, so at least (p − 1)! in size, and also at most A · Cᵖ, which factorials outrun; both proofs trap a whole number where no whole number can be","w":["J would be a fraction with bottom p; both proofs divide by p","J would be 0 for every prime p; both proofs show a sum wanders","J would be negative; both proofs use the quadratic formula"],"why":"23g4: every slope of f at 1, …, n is a multiple of p!, and at 0 all but one are, so J/(p − 1)! is whole and not a multiple of p."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["23h"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["23h"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"23h1.1","q":"How is the sum of f about a new centre w found, and why is the result the matching sum?","a":"write x = w + h and turn f(w + h) into sums in h that are known; it reaches as far as those sums do; one total near w has only one power sum about w (23e3), so it is the matching sum","w":["take every slope of f at w afresh; no shorter way gives the matching sum","put x − w for x in the sum about 0; it is the matching sum because the coefficients are the same","write x = w + h; the result is one of many power sums about w with that total"],"why":"eˣ = e^w·e^h gives Σ e^w (x − w)ᵏ/k!."},
 {"u":"23h2.1","q":"What is (1 + x)^α as a power sum, and how does the proof find the total without bounding a slope?","a":"Σ (α choose k) xᵏ for |x| < 1, with α choose k = α(α − 1)⋯(α − k + 1)/k!; term by term, (1 + x)S′ = αS, so S(x)(1 + x)^(−α) has slope 0 and stays at its value 1 at 0","w":["Σ αᵏxᵏ/k! for every x; the error ceiling shows it","Σ (α choose k) xᵏ for every x; the pieces stop after x^α for every real α","Σ (α choose k) xᵏ for |x| < 1; the bracket technique fences the leftover"],"why":"Zero slope means flat (14d). For whole α the pieces stop and it is 4k's two-term power."},
 {"u":"23h3.1","q":"Which moves build new power sums from old, and why is a sum built this way the matching sum?","a":"put cxᵐ in for x, multiply by a power of x, add, or take slopes and areas term by term, wherever every sum used settles; a function has only one power sum about a point (23e3), so no slope of the new function need be found","w":["only adding; a sum built by putting x² for x is not the matching sum","any move at all, at every x; the built sum is the matching sum because it has the same first term","the same moves; the built sum must still be checked slope by slope"],"why":"e^(−x²) = Σ (−x²)ᵏ/k! comes with no slope taken."},
 {"u":"23h4.1","q":"Where Σ uₖxᵏ and Σ vₖxᵏ both settle in size, what is the number in front of xᵏ in their product?","a":"cₖ = u₀vₖ + u₁vₖ₋₁ + ⋯ + uₖv₀: multiply as for polynomials and gather every pair of powers that adds to k","w":["cₖ = uₖvₖ","cₖ = (u₀ + ⋯ + uₖ)(v₀ + ⋯ + vₖ)","cₖ = uₖ + vₖ"],"why":"Settling in size lets the pairs be gathered in any order."},
 {"u":"23h5.1","q":"What is out of place in “√4 = √(1 + 3) = 1 + 3/2 − 9/8 + 27/16 − ⋯”, and in “(Σ uₖxᵏ)(Σ vₖxᵏ) = Σ uₖvₖxᵏ”?","a":"the binomial sum reaches 1; at x = 3 its pieces grow and it wanders; and the piece with xᵏ gathers every pair j + l = k, not only j = l = k","w":["the binomial sum needs a whole power; and the product has pieces uₖ + vₖ","nothing in the first, the sum settles slowly at 2; in the second the pieces are uₖvₖx²ᵏ","the coefficients are wrong; and the product is right when both sums settle in size"],"why":"A sum is used inside its reach, and multiplied as polynomials are."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["23i"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["23i"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"23i1.1","q":"Which fence fits which leftover, and why did six pieces give ln 2 where two million did not?","a":"the bracket when signs take turns and sizes fall, a geometric sum when each piece is at most k times the one before, the error ceiling otherwise, and choose the sum whose pieces shrink fastest; 1 − ½ + ⅓ − ⋯ leaves about 1/(n + 1), while 2(⅓ + 1/(3·3³) + ⋯) shrinks by a ninth each piece","w":["always the error ceiling; six pieces sufficed because the bracket is tighter","the bracket for every sum; two million pieces were needed because the fence was badly chosen","a geometric sum for every sum; six pieces sufficed because ln 2 is small"],"why":"The fence was never the hard part. The choice of sum was."},
 {"u":"23i2.1","q":"When is a total known to d places, and why is a fence narrower than half a unit in the last place not enough?","a":"when every number in its fence, with the slips of rounding added, rounds to the same d places; a narrow fence can still straddle the boundary halfway between two roundings","w":["when the next piece is under half a unit in the last place; nothing more is needed","when the fence is narrower than one unit in the last place; a half unit is too strict","when two computations agree to d places; a narrow fence may still be wrong"],"why":"Report only the places on which the whole fence agrees."},
 {"u":"23i3.1","q":"How close is sin θ to θ and cos θ to 1 − θ²/2 for |θ| ≤ h in radians, and why is the error one power smaller than it looks?","a":"sin θ is θ to within h³/6 and cos θ is 1 − θ²/2 to within h⁴/24; sin has no θ² piece, so its match of degree 1 is already its match of degree 2, and the error is that of degree 2","w":["sin θ is θ to within h²/2 and cos θ is 1 to within h²/2; the error is as it looks","sin θ is θ to within h³/6, in degrees as well; the error is smaller because sin is odd in degrees","sin θ is θ exactly for small θ; there is no error"],"why":"T₁ = T₂ = θ for sin, and R₂ carries θ³/3!."},
 {"u":"23i4.1","q":"What is done to the input before any pieces are added, and which numbers then need more places?","a":"it is moved close to the centre with exact facts: whole half turns off an angle, a power of 2 split off inside a log, an exponent split into a whole part and a small part; only the facts used to move it, like π or ln 2, need to be known to more places","w":["it is rounded to two places; then every piece needs more places","nothing; more pieces are added until the fence closes","it is moved away from the centre, where the pieces are larger; then the coefficients need more places"],"why":"Near the centre the sums shrink fast from the first piece."},
 {"u":"23i5.1","q":"What is out of place in “1/10! < 0.0000003, so ten pieces give e to within 0.0000003”, and in “the fence is [0.1234564, 0.1234566], narrower than 0.0000005, so six places are known”?","a":"the leftover is the whole tail, not only the next piece, and the slips of rounding add to it; and that fence straddles 0.1234565, the boundary between rounding to 0.123456 and to 0.123457","w":["1/10! is larger than 0.0000003; and the fence is wider than 0.0000005","nothing in the first; in the second seven places are known","the next piece should be 1/11!; and a fence gives places only when it is wider than a unit"],"why":"A tail is fenced, and a rounding is read off the whole fence."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["23j"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["23j"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"23j1.1","q":"What is the storing function of a list, and what does it give back?","a":"A(x) = Σ uₖxᵏ wherever it settles; if its reach is above 0 the function alone gives back the list, uₖ = A⁽ᵏ⁾(0)/k!, so two lists whose storing functions agree near 0 are the same list","w":["A(x) = Σ uₖ; it gives back only the total of the list","A(x) = Σ uₖxᵏ; it gives back the list only if the list is finite","A(x) = Σ xᵏ/uₖ; two different lists can share one storing function near 0"],"why":"It is 23e3's smooth-sum technique, used as a filing system."},
 {"u":"23j2.1","q":"How is Fₙ = (φⁿ − ψⁿ)/√5 reached for the rabbit counts?","a":"store the list in G(x) = Σ Fₖxᵏ, turn the technique Fₙ = Fₙ₋₁ + Fₙ₋₂ into an equation, G(x) = x/(1 − x − x²), split it over the roots φ and ψ into two geometric sums, and read back the number in front of xⁿ","w":["guess the formula from the first eight counts and check two more","divide each count by the one before until the ratio is φ, then take the nth power","sum the counts to n and subtract the sum to n − 1"],"why":"Whole numbers come out of square roots of 5: Fₙ is the whole number nearest φⁿ/√5."},
 {"u":"23j3.1","q":"Read for storing functions, what does the number in front of xⁿ in A(x)B(x) count?","a":"cₙ = u₀vₙ + u₁vₙ₋₁ + ⋯ + uₙv₀: every way to split n into a part from A and a part from B","w":["uₙvₙ: the ways to take n from both","uₙ + vₙ: the ways to take n from one or the other","the ways to choose n of the first n pieces"],"why":"So counting in two stages is multiplying the storing functions."},
 {"u":"23j4.1","q":"What is out of place in “G(1) = −1, so 0 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 3 + ⋯ = −1”, and in “A(0) = B(0), so the two lists are the same”?","a":"1 lies outside the reach 1/φ ≈ 0.618, where the sum wanders and the formula says nothing about it; and A(0) = B(0) matches only the first entries: the functions must agree near 0","w":["G(1) is 1, not −1; and the lists are the same when A(1) = B(1)","nothing in the first; in the second the lists must be finite","the sum is −1 only after reordering; and A(0) is never B(0)"],"why":"A storing function speaks for its list only inside its reach."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["23k"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["23k"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"23k1.1","q":"What does an expansion of f as x shrinks promise, how is that unlike a sum that settles, and where should it be cut?","a":"for every n, stopping before uₙxⁿ leaves an error at most C·xⁿ for small x: a promise for each fixed n as x shrinks, not for fixed x as n grows, and the sum need not settle anywhere but 0; when each error is at most the next piece and the pieces shrink and then grow, stop just before the smallest piece","w":["it promises the sum settles at f(x) for small x; cut it as late as possible","it promises nothing, since the sum wanders; it should not be used","it promises an error at most C·xⁿ; cut it after the largest piece"],"why":"For f(x) = ∫₀^∞ e^(−t)/(1 + xt) dt the error after n pieces is at most n!xⁿ, yet the sum wanders for every x > 0."},
 {"u":"23k2.1","q":"What does a bound on the second slope of ln g at its top give, what does it give for n!, and what had 22k3's fence missed?","a":"with (ln g)″ ≥ −K on a stretch of width w beside the top m, g stays above e^(−Kw²/2)·g(m) there, so the area is at least w·e^(−Kw²/2)·g(m); for n! = ∫ tⁿe^(−t) it gives n! ≥ √n·e^(−½)(n/e)ⁿ; 22k3 missed the width √n of the hill","w":["the area equals g(m); for n! it gives (n/e)ⁿ exactly; 22k3 missed nothing","the area is at most w·g(m); for n! it gives n! ≤ n(n/e)ⁿ; 22k3 missed the factor e","g stays below e^(Kw²)·g(m); for n! it gives n! ≥ nⁿ; 22k3 missed the height of the hill"],"why":"Near a smooth top a curve falls away like the square of the distance."},
 {"u":"23k3.1","q":"What is shown about dₙ = ln n! − (n + ½) ln n + n, and what do the slivers sₖ measure?","a":"dₙ falls and settles at some c, with c < dₙ < c + 1/(8n); sₖ is the area between ln and its chord on [k, k + 1], above 0 and at most 1/(8k(k + 1))","w":["dₙ climbs past every ceiling; sₖ is the area under ln on [k, k + 1]","dₙ falls and settles at 0; sₖ is the kth piece of ln n!","dₙ is the same for every n; sₖ is 0, since chords lie on the curve"],"why":"ln bends down: its chord lies under it and its tangent line at k + ½ over it."},
 {"u":"23k4.1","q":"What is the size of n!, and where does the constant √(2π) come from?","a":"n! ~ √(2πn)(n/e)ⁿ, with √(2πn)(n/e)ⁿ < n! < √(2πn)(n/e)ⁿ·e^(1/(8n)); the constant comes from the areas under powers of sin on [0, π/2], which know π","w":["n! ~ (n/e)ⁿ; the constant comes from the reach of the sum for eˣ","n! ~ √(2πn)(n/e)ⁿ; the constant comes from a computer's match to many places","n! = √(2πn)(n/e)ⁿ exactly; the constant comes from the circle of radius n"],"why":"e^c = √(2π), with c the limit of dₙ from 23k3."},
 {"u":"23k5.1","q":"What is out of place in “n! ~ √(2πn)(n/e)ⁿ, so n! − √(2πn)(n/e)ⁿ → 0”, and in “more pieces of an expansion always do better”?","a":"~ says the quotient heads to 1; the difference here passes every ceiling (22k4); and an expansion's pieces shrink and then grow: at x = 0.1 nine pieces give f to within 0.0004 and thirty give −65","w":["the difference heads to 1; and more pieces do better once n passes 10","nothing in the first; in the second the pieces must be taken in pairs","~ says the two are equal from some n on; and an expansion settles, slowly"],"why":"Growing alike is about ratios, and an expansion is cut at its smallest piece."});
